message:
I see a potential issue in the padded version. In the first `while(true)` loop:

```cpp
while (true) {
   if (it >= end) [[unlikely]] {
      ctx.error = error_code::unexpected_end;
      return;
   }

   uint64_t chunk;
   std::memcpy(&chunk, it, 8);
   ...
}
```

The check `it >= end` ensures `it < end`, but then `memcpy(&chunk, it, 8)` reads 8 bytes from `it`. Since `end = buffer.size() - 16` (padding_bytes = 16), and `it < end`, we have `it + 8 <= end + 7 < buffer.size()`. So this is safe.

But wait - in the decode loop:
```cpp
std::memcpy(p, start, 8);
```

This reads 8 bytes from `start`. `start` can be up to `it - 1` (since the loop breaks when `start >= it`). And `it` points to the closing quote. So `start + 8 <= it + 7`. Since `it < end` and `end = buffer.size() - 16`, we have `start + 8 <= end + 7 < buffer.size()`. Safe.

Hmm, let me think about this differently. The issue says "READ 5". Let me look at the `handle_unicode_code_point` check again: `if (it + 4 >= end)`. This means we proceed when `it + 4 < end`, i.e., `it + 4 <= end - 1`. So `it + 3 <= end - 2`. `hex_to_u32` reads `it[0]` through `it[3]`, which is safe.

Wait, but what if `end` is the actual end of the buffer (non-padded case)? Then `it + 4 < end` means `it + 3 <= end - 2`, so `it[3]` is at `end - 2`, which is within bounds. Safe.

Let me look at the `skip_unicode_code_point` function again. Actually, let me look at the `skip_string` function in the non-padded version more carefully. The issue might be in the `skip_string_view` function:

```cpp
template <auto Opts>
GLZ_ALWAYS_INLINE void skip_string_view(is_context auto&& ctx, auto&& it, auto&& end) noexcept
{
   while (it < end) [[likely]] {
      const auto* pc = std::memchr(it, '"', size_t(end - it));
      if (pc) [[likely]] {
         it = reinterpret_cast<std::decay_t<decltype(it)>>(pc);
         auto* prev = it - 1;
         while (*prev == '\\') {
            --prev;
         }
         ...
```

Here, `prev = it - 1`. If `it` points to the first character of the buffer (i.e., `it == buffer.data()`), then `it - 1` is out of bounds! But wait, `it` points to the quote character, and the string starts with `"`, so `it` would be at the opening quote position. `it - 1` would be before the opening quote. If the opening quote is at position 0, then `it - 1` is out of bounds.

But actually, `skip_string_view` is called after the opening quote has been consumed (via `++it` in `skip_string`). So `it` points to the first character after the opening quote. `it - 1` points to the opening quote, which is in bounds.

Hmm, let me think about this differently. Let me look at the `skip_string` function in the non-padded version:

```cpp
template <auto Opts>
   requires(not check_is_padded(Opts))
GLZ_ALWAYS_INLINE void skip_string(is_context auto&& ctx, auto&& it, auto&& end) noexcept
{
   if constexpr (!check_opening_handled(Opts)) {
      ++it;
   }

   if constexpr (check_validate_skipped(Opts)) {
      while (true) {
         if ((*it & 0b11100000) == 0) [[unlikely]] {
            ctx.error = error_code::syntax_error;
            return;
         }

         switch (*it) {
         case '"': {
            ++it;
            return;
         }
         case '\\': {
            ++it;
            if (char_unescape_table[uint8_t(*it)]) {
               ++it;
               continue;
            }
            else if (*it == 'u') {
               ++it;
               if (skip_unicode_code_point(it, end)) [[likely]] {
                  continue;
               }
               ...
```

This reads `*it` without checking if `it < end`. If the input is malformed (e.g., a string that doesn't end with a quote), this could read past the end. But this is a read of 1 byte, not 5.

Let me look at the `skip_unicode_code_point` function again. The check is `if (it + 4 >= end)`. Wait, I need to be more careful. `it + 4 >= end` means we return false when `it + 4 >= end`. So we proceed when `it + 4 < end`. That means `it + 4 <= end - 1`, so `it + 3 <= end - 2`. `hex_to_u32(it)` reads `it[0]` through `it[3]`, which is safe.

But wait - what if `end` is the actual end of the buffer (non-padded)? Then `it + 4 < end` means `it + 3 <= end - 2`. So `it[3]` is at most `end - 2`. Safe.

Hmm, let me look at the `skip_unicode_code_point` surrogate pair case again:

```cpp
if (it + 6 >= end) [[unlikely]] {
   return false;
}
```

We proceed when `it + 6 < end`, i.e., `it + 5 <= end - 1`. Then `std::memcpy(&u, it, 2)` reads `it[0]` and `it[1]`, safe. Then `it += 2`, and `hex_to_u32(it)` reads `it[0]` through `it[3]`. Since `it + 5 <= end - 1`, we have `it + 3 <= end - 3`, safe.

